description:
The custom floor_char_boundary polyfill in posting.rs (added in round 2 fix commit 8b2a449) has incorrect semantics. It uses take_while(|(i, _)| *i < max_bytes) which checks the START of each character against max_bytes, then adds the character's full byte length. When a multi-byte character starts before max_bytes but extends past it, the function includes the entire character and returns a value GREATER than max_bytes.

Examples:
- floor_char_boundary("héllo", 2) returns 3 (should be 1)
- floor_char_boundary("a世b", 2) returns 4 (should be 1)  
- floor_char_boundary("😀abc", 2) returns 4 (should be 0)

This violates the docstring contract ('largest byte index <= max_bytes') and the semantics of str::floor_char_boundary that it claims to replicate.

Practical impact: abstract truncation in posting.rs and delivery.rs may exceed the 200-byte target, but won't panic because the returned index never exceeds s.len().

Fix: Either use str::floor_char_boundary directly (stabilized in Rust 1.80, which matches the project MSRV), or fix the implementation:

  fn floor_char_boundary(s: &str, max_bytes: usize) -> usize {
      if max_bytes >= s.len() { return s.len(); }
      let mut i = max_bytes;
      while i > 0 && !s.is_char_boundary(i) { i -= 1; }
      i
  }

Affected files: src/posting.rs (lines 86-95), src/delivery.rs (lines 350-351), src/posting.rs (lines 1224-1225).
